{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Mount /tmp on node1 with execution, setuid behavior, and device-node use disabled.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before the change, /tmp was part of the root filesystem. Final observations show it as a separate tmpfs mounted with nosuid,nodev,noexec. Execution was rejected, device-node access was denied, and the mount remained writable with mode 1777.","evidence_ids":["cmd-14fdb65079d24417","cmd-1982c18f451e4939","cmd-e0a881510cc04745"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Mount /dev/shm on node1 with execution, setuid behavior, and device-node use disabled.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final /dev/shm tmpfs mount has nosuid,nodev,noexec. Execution was rejected, device-node access was denied, and the mount remained writable with mode 1777.","evidence_ids":["cmd-14fdb65079d24417","cmd-1982c18f451e4939","cmd-e0a881510cc04745"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Ensure the hardened settings for both mounts survive a reboot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The pre-restart boot was healthy, the restart was acknowledged, and the boot identity changed. After recovery, the system was running and both mounts retained nosuid,nodev,noexec; their fstab entries remained present and functional checks passed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ffe3a3e14e3e46c2","cmd-4d14d54f72314af9","cmd-694be0151fa74462","cmd-1a52138cdf4b4a53","cmd-e0a881510cc04745","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"The implementation, activation, probes, and reboot were causally connected to the requested outcome or mandatory restart evidence. Temporary execution, device, and write probes were removed. Final lifecycle observations show no failed services, no new network exposure, and healthy package state. One minor unnecessary retained mutation is evident: cmd-3b22dce9c76f4074 created /etc/fstab.pre-mount-hardening as a prudent rollback copy, but the trace does not show it being removed after successful validation; it therefore remains potential backup residue. Reboot-related /tmp turnover and changed process IDs are expected, not hygiene defects.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3b22dce9c76f4074","cmd-b8e3427528754cfa","cmd-e0a881510cc04745","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. Both /tmp and /dev/shm are hardened with nosuid,nodev,noexec, and a directly observed reboot demonstrates persistence and healthy recovery. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete and agrees with the fixed boot-identity snapshots.","limitations":["The nosuid property was directly observed in live mount options before and after reboot, but there was no behavioral setuid execution probe; its enforcement is therefore supported by kernel-reported mount state rather than an explicit setuid test.","The retained status of /etc/fstab.pre-mount-hardening is inferred from its creation and the absence of a cleanup command; lifecycle snapshots did not inventory /etc."]}
